      "text": "To determine how many integers Josef could pick such that Timothy's quotient is an integer, we need to understand when 1000 divided by an integer \\( n \\) results in an integer. This occurs if and only if \\( n \\) is a divisor of 1000. Thus, the problem boils down to counting the number of divisors of 1000.\n\nFirst, we need to find the prime factorization of 1000.\n\n\\[ 1000 = 10^3 = (2 \\times 5)^3 = 2^3 \\times 5^3 \\]\n\nThe formula to find the number of divisors of a number \\( n = p_1^{e_1} \\times p_2^{e_2} \\times \\cdots \\times p_k^{e_k} \\) is:\n\n\\[ (e_1 + 1)(e_2 + 1) \\cdots (e_k + 1) \\]\n\nFor \\( 1000 = 2^3 \\times 5^3 \\):\n\n\\[ (3 + 1)(3 + 1) = 4 \\times 4 = 16 \\]\n\nSo, there are 16 divisors of 1000. Thus, Josef can pick 16 different integers such that Timothy's quotient is an integer. The answer is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{16} \\]\n\nWe can verify this through a simple Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Function to find the number of divisors of a number\r\ndef count_divisors(n):\r\n    count = 0\r\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\r\n        if n % i == 0:\r\n            count += 1\r\n    return count\r\n\r\n# Compute the number of divisors of 1000\r\nnumber = 1000\r\ndivisors_count = count_divisors(number)\r\nprint(divisors_count)\n```",
